        <!-- Section 5: Common Safe Zone Mistakes -->
        <section class="editorial-block">
          <h2>5 Common Reels Safe Zone Mistakes and How to Avoid Them</h2>
          <p>
            Even seasoned content creators regularly make layout mistakes that hurt their reach, watch time, and click-through rates. Here are the five most frequent pitfalls and how to steer clear of them:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Placing Auto-Captions at the Bottom Center:</strong> Third-party auto-caption apps and AI generators frequently default subtitle placement to the bottom 10% to 15% of the screen. Always nudge your subtitle track upward in your editor timeline so it rests roughly 25% to 35% above the bottom edge. This ensures complete clearance above the creator username, caption text, and audio track banner.</li>
            <li><strong>Ignoring the 1:1 Profile Grid Cutoff:</strong> If your Reel cover contains headline text near the top or bottom of the 9:16 frame, your profile grid will display chopped words like "OW TO GROW" instead of "HOW TO GROW ON INSTAGRAM". Always verify that your thumbnail focal point and headline fit squarely inside the central 1080 × 1080 pixel box.</li>
            <li><strong>Stretching Wide Headlines to the Right Margin:</strong> Keep headlines compact and centered. While full-width headlines look fine in desktop editors, Instagram's right-hand action column (like, comment, share, and audio disc) will overlap and obscure the rightmost 2 to 3 words on mobile devices. Keep headline width under 780 pixels.</li>
            <li><strong>Assuming All Smartphone Screens Are Identical:</strong> Older iPhones use a standard 16:9 ratio (such as iPhone SE and iPhone 8), while modern flagship devices feature taller 19.5:9 or 20:9 displays (iPhone 15 Pro, Samsung Galaxy S24 Ultra). Furthermore, modern devices have rounded screen corners and dynamic island/notch cutouts. Maintaining a comfortable safety buffer around all four margins accommodates varying hardware display shapes.</li>
            <li><strong>Using Low-Contrast Subtitles Without Outlines:</strong> White text over bright video backgrounds disappears even inside the safe zone. Always apply a 3px to 4px dark stroke outline, a subtle drop shadow, or a translucent dark background pill behind on-screen text to guarantee legibility across dynamic video scenes.</li>
          </ul>
